    {
      "id": "02",
      "title": "def or async def?",
      "question": "Should an endpoint be async def or def, and what happens if you get it wrong?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "Question two. Should an endpoint be an async function, or a plain synchronous one? And what happens if you get it wrong? Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "<em>async def</em> or <em>def</em>, and what if you choose wrong?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "graph",
          "say": "An async endpoint runs directly on the event loop, alongside every other request on that worker. A plain synchronous endpoint is sent to a thread pool and awaited, so blocking calls there are fine. Now the mistake: a blocking call, like requests dot get or time dot sleep, inside an async endpoint. It never yields, so the event loop freezes, and every other request on that worker waits. The thread pool has limits too: forty threads by default, so the forty-first concurrent blocking call waits for a free thread.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Event loop or <em>thread pool</em>",
            "nodes": [
              {
                "id": "a",
                "label": "async def /fast",
                "x": 220,
                "y": 290,
                "w": 290,
                "h": 64
              },
              {
                "id": "d",
                "label": "def /legacy",
                "x": 220,
                "y": 650,
                "w": 290,
                "h": 64
              },
              {
                "id": "bad",
                "label": "async def + requests.get",
                "x": 250,
                "y": 470,
                "w": 410,
                "h": 64,
                "hidden": true
              },
              {
                "id": "loop",
                "label": "event loop",
                "x": 760,
                "y": 290,
                "w": 230,
                "h": 70
              },
              {
                "id": "pool",
                "label": "thread pool · 40",
                "x": 760,
                "y": 650,
                "w": 260,
                "h": 70
              }
            ],
            "edges": [
              {
                "from": "a",
                "to": "loop"
              },
              {
                "from": "d",
                "to": "pool"
              }
            ],
            "panels": [
              {
                "key": "state",
                "label": "Worker",
                "type": "list",
                "rows": 1,
                "initial": [
                  "serving"
                ]
              }
            ],
            "steps": [
              {
                "at": "directly",
                "nodes": {
                  "a": "active",
                  "loop": "active"
                },
                "edges": {
                  "a-loop": "relax"
                },
                "note": "async def → runs on the event loop"
              },
              {
                "at": "pool",
                "nodes": {
                  "a": "base",
                  "loop": "base",
                  "d": "active",
                  "pool": "active"
                },
                "edges": {
                  "a-loop": "base",
                  "d-pool": "relax"
                },
                "note": "def → thread pool, awaited"
              },
              {
                "at": "mistake",
                "show": [
                  "bad"
                ],
                "add": [
                  {
                    "from": "bad",
                    "to": "loop"
                  }
                ],
                "nodes": {
                  "d": "base",
                  "pool": "base",
                  "bad": "queued"
                },
                "edges": {
                  "d-pool": "base",
                  "bad-loop": "relax"
                },
                "note": "Blocking call inside async def…"
              },
              {
                "at": "freezes",
                "nodes": {
                  "loop": "dim",
                  "a": "dim"
                },
                "edges": {
                  "a-loop": "dim"
                },
                "panels": {
                  "state": [
                    "frozen: every request waits"
                  ]
                },
                "note": "…never yields: the loop freezes"
              },
              {
                "at": "limits",
                "nodes": {
                  "pool": "queued",
                  "loop": "base",
                  "a": "base",
                  "bad": "dim"
                },
                "edges": {
                  "a-loop": "base",
                  "bad-loop": "dim"
                },
                "panels": {
                  "state": [
                    "41st blocking call waits"
                  ]
                },
                "note": "Thread pool: 40 tokens per worker"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "compare",
          "say": "So: write async endpoints only when everything you call is awaitable, like an async HTTP client or database driver. If you call blocking libraries, write a plain function, and let the thread pool handle it. And CPU-heavy work belongs in a process pool, or a task queue, not in either.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Match the endpoint to <em>what it calls</em>",
            "lang": "python",
            "left": {
              "at": "awaitable",
              "label": "async def",
              "title": "Everything awaited",
              "tone": "good",
              "code": "async def get(url):\n    async with httpx.AsyncClient() as c:\n        return (await c.get(url)).json()"
            },
            "right": {
              "at": "blocking",
              "label": "def",
              "title": "Blocking libraries",
              "tone": "good",
              "code": "def get(url):\n    return requests.get(url).json()\n# runs in the thread pool"
            },
            "verdict": {
              "at": "process",
              "text": "Unsure? Plain def. CPU work: a process pool or a queue.",
              "code": "never block the loop"
            }
          }
        }
      ]
